questions.question:
Zenith Garments Ltd., an Indian resident corporate entity, avails a foreign currency term loan of USD 10,000,000 from Apex Bank PLC, London, incurring an annual interest liability of USD 600,000 for P.Y. 2024-25. Zenith Garments Ltd. deploys the borrowed funds as follows:
(i) USD 6,000,000 is utilized for setting up and operating a ready-made garment manufacturing plant in Dhaka, Bangladesh;
(ii) USD 2,500,000 is invested in foreign corporate bonds issued by a Singapore company (earning interest income outside India);
(iii) USD 1,500,000 is utilized for expanding its domestic textile processing mill in Surat, Gujarat.
What portion of the interest paid (USD 600,000) by Zenith Garments Ltd. to Apex Bank PLC, London, is deemed to accrue or arise in India under Section 9(1)(v) of the Income-tax Act, 1961?

Reliance Infrastructures Ltd., an Indian resident company, enters into a syndicated loan arrangement with a consortium of European banks led by Deutsche Capital GmbH (Germany) for financing a highway project in Rajasthan. In P.Y. 2024-25, the company pays the following amounts to Deutsche Capital GmbH:
(1) Loan arrangement and processing fee: INR 25,00,000;
(2) Commitment fee on the unutilized portion of the sanctioned credit line: INR 15,00,000;
(3) Regular interest on the drawn loan balance: INR 85,00,000.
Deutsche Capital GmbH argues that the commitment fee on unutilized credit and the arrangement fee are fees for managerial/technical services (FTS) and, since no technical personnel visited India, they cannot be taxed as interest under Section 9(1)(v). Which of the following statements correctly characterizes the nature and sourcing of these payments under the Income-tax Act, 1961?

Paramount Worldwide LLC, a non-resident entertainment conglomerate based in California, grants the following rights to Zee Star Media Ltd., an Indian broadcasting company, during P.Y. 2024-25:
(i) Theatrical distribution and exhibition rights of 5 Hollywood cinematographic feature films across cinema multiplexes in India for INR 1,50,00,000;
(ii) Television broadcasting rights and video tape broadcasting rights for 3 television serials over satellite channels in India for INR 75,00,000;
(iii) Outright and permanent assignment of complete copyright in an original literary manuscript written by an American author with all rights extinguished permanently for a lump-sum consideration of INR 30,00,000 (chargeable under the head 'Capital Gains').
Which of the payments received by Paramount Worldwide LLC constitute 'Royalty' within the meaning of Explanation 2 to Section 9(1)(vi) of the Income-tax Act, 1961?

FinSecure Ltd., an Indian fintech company, enters into a service contract with CloudHost Inc., a US cloud infrastructure company. Under the contract, FinSecure is granted access to high-security dedicated cryptographic hardware security modules (HSMs) located exclusively in a data centre in Virginia, USA. The servers and encryption keys are operated remotely by FinSecure's engineers via secure API tunnels. CloudHost Inc. argues that:
(i) The physical equipment is located in the USA, not India;
(ii) Possession and physical control of the equipment remain entirely with CloudHost in Virginia;
(iii) FinSecure does not have direct physical custody of the servers.
Based on this, CloudHost claims the hosting fees of USD 120,000 cannot be treated as royalty under Section 9(1)(vi). How does Explanation 5 to Section 9(1)(vi) address CloudHost Inc.'s arguments?

Mr. Kevin, a non-resident individual (citizen of Canada), earned or received the following amounts during P.Y. 2024-25:
(1) Dividend received in Toronto from Infosys Ltd., an Indian company: INR 45,000;
(2) Interest received in New York from an Indian resident, Mr. Alok, on money borrowed by Alok and used exclusively for running a restaurant in London: INR 80,000;
(3) Royalty received in Canada from the Government of India for the use of a patented water purification technology in Rajasthan: INR 1,20,000;
(4) Fees for technical services received in Canada from a French company (non-resident) for engineering consultancy services utilized in an oil refinery operated by the French company in Jamnagar, Gujarat: INR 1,50,000;
(5) Cash gift received in Toronto from an Indian resident friend (not a relative): INR 75,000.
Compute the Total Income chargeable to tax in India in the hands of Mr. Kevin for Assessment Year 2025-26 under the regular provisions of the Income-tax Act, 1961.

Vanguard Orient Fund, an offshore fund incorporated in Mauritius on 1st June 2023, has a total corpus of INR 300 crore. During P.Y. 2024-25, the fund's monthly average corpus stood at INR 240 crore. The fund made the following investments:
(1) Invested INR 50 crore (16.67% of corpus) in equity shares of Sun Pharma Ltd.;
(2) Invested INR 25 crore in Zenora Biotech Ltd., a company in which a director of Vanguard Orient Fund holds an 18% equity interest;
(3) Invested INR 45 crore (15% of corpus) in Tata Motors Ltd.
Does Vanguard Orient Fund satisfy the conditions laid down in Section 9A(3) for being treated as an Eligible Investment Fund?

Gujarat International Finance Tec-City (IFSC) hosts Beacon Capital Management, a fund manager that commenced operations in the IFSC on 1st November 2023. Beacon manages Trident Ocean Fund, an offshore fund. Trident Ocean Fund has only 12 unconnected members, and its single largest investor holds a 35% participation interest. Furthermore, Beacon exercises a contractual monitoring mechanism over Indian investee companies that includes the right to appoint nominee directors to protect the fund's investment. The Assessing Officer claims the fund is ineligible under Section 9A because:
(1) It has fewer than 25 members [violating Section 9A(3)(e)];
(2) A single member holds > 10% [violating Section 9A(3)(f)];
(3) Appointing nominee directors amounts to participating in the day-to-day operations of business in India [violating Section 9A(3)(k)].
How does Notification No. 59/2022 issued under Section 9A(8A) resolve these issues for an IFSC-based fund manager?

Neptune Global Fund is an offshore fund incorporated in Singapore that fully satisfies all conditions of an Eligible Investment Fund under Section 9A(3) and acts through an Eligible Fund Manager in Mumbai. During P.Y. 2024-25, the fund earns:
(1) Short-term capital gains of INR 1,20,00,000 on sale of listed equity shares of Indian companies through a recognized stock exchange in India;
(2) Dividend income of INR 30,00,000 from Indian companies;
(3) Capital gains of INR 2,50,00,000 on transfer of bonds issued by the Government of Indonesia sold in Singapore.
The fund claims that since it qualifies for the safe harbour under Section 9A, all its income (including Indian capital gains and dividends) is completely exempt from tax in India. Evaluate the fund's claim in light of Section 9A(6) of the Income-tax Act, 1961.

Peeyush, an Indian citizen who had resided in the UK for 20 years, returned to India on 12th June 2025 for permanent settlement. For the Previous Year 2025-26, his physical stay in India was 293 days. He exercised the option to shift out of the default tax regime under Section 115BAC. His receipts during P.Y. 2025-26 were as follows:
(i) Rental income of ₹1,80,000 from a flat in London deposited in a UK bank account (let out from July 2025);
(ii) Dividends of ₹1,20,000 on shares of German companies collected in London and retained there with RBI permission;
(iii) Son A (aged 12) stayed in London: earned foreign currency income of ₹75,000 in the UK and ₹20,000 in India;
(iv) Son B (aged 19) stayed in London: earned foreign currency income of ₹75,000 in the UK and ₹20,000 in India;
(v) Repatriation of ₹50,000 into India during P.Y. 2025-26, being capital gains earned in the preceding accounting year when he was a non-resident.
Determine the residential status and Total Income of Peeyush for Assessment Year 2026-27.

Mr. David, an Indian citizen serving in the Ministry of External Affairs, Government of India, was posted to the Indian Embassy in Ottawa, Canada, on 31.03.2025. He did not visit India at any time during the Previous Year 2025-26. His receipts for P.Y. 2025-26 were:
(i) Salary from the Government of India (computed): ₹5,00,000;
(ii) Foreign Allowance granted by the Government: ₹4,00,000;
(iii) Interest on fixed deposits with a bank in India: ₹1,00,000;
(iv) Agricultural income earned in Country X: ₹2,00,000;
(v) Rental income from house property located in Country X: ₹2,50,000.
Compute Mr. David's Gross Total Income for Assessment Year 2026-27.

Examine the taxability of the following transactions in the hands of the non-resident recipients under Section 9 of the Income-tax Act, 1961:
(I) A non-resident German engineering company with no PE in India received 'Engineering Fees' for providing drawings and designs utilized for executing electrical work in India;
(II) A Mumbai law firm engaged a UK barrister to argue a litigation matter before the Supreme Court of India and remitted professional fees of £5,000;
(III) The Government of India paid ₹50 lakhs to Mr. Klaus (a non-resident) for the acquisition and use of a patented green-hydrogen process;
(IV) Sai Engineering, a non-resident foreign company, received 8% debentures worth ₹20 lakhs from an Indian corporate in consideration for providing technical know-how used in Mumbai.
Which of the above receipts attract income tax in India under Section 9?

Singtel Ltd. (incorporated in Singapore, 55% held by Godavari (P) Ltd., an Indian company) reported the following data for P.Y. 2025-26:
- Passive income is ₹50 crores out of total income of ₹110 crores (45.45%);
- Assets at depreciated/book values: India ₹210 crores (Fixed 120 + Intangibles 50 + Other 40); Singapore ₹400 crores (Fixed 80 + Intangibles 200 + Other 120); Total ₹610 crores;
- Employees: 70 resident in India; 90 resident in Singapore; Total 160 employees;
- Payroll expenses: India ₹8 crores; Singapore ₹12 crores; Total ₹20 crores;
- Board meetings: 8 board meetings held during the year, out of which 3 were held in India and 5 in Singapore.
Determine the residential status of Singtel Ltd. for Assessment Year 2026-27.

STYLE Inc., a notified Foreign Institutional Investor (FII), derived the following incomes during Financial Year 2025-26:
(1) Dividend from listed shares of Indian companies: ₹6,20,000;
(2) Interest on securities: ₹17,32,000 (expenses incurred in earning such interest: ₹26,000);
(3) Capital gains on sale of securities:
    - Long-term capital gains on sale of bonds of J Ltd.: Sale ₹47,00,000; Cost ₹32,00,000 (purchased May 2018, sold March 2026; CII 2018-19: 280, 2025-26: 376);
    - Short-term capital gains on sale of listed equity shares of E Ltd. (STT paid at purchase and sale): Sale ₹12,40,000; Cost ₹7,80,000;
    - Short-term capital gains on sale of unlisted equity shares of M Ltd.: Sale ₹8,40,000; Cost ₹3,72,000.
Compute the Total Income of STYLE Inc. under Section 115AD for Assessment Year 2026-27.

STYLE Inc., a notified Foreign Institutional Investor (FII), has a computed Total Income of ₹47,80,000 for A.Y. 2026-27, comprising:
- Dividend and interest on securities: ₹23,52,000;
- Long-term capital gains on sale of bonds of J Ltd.: ₹15,00,000;
- Short-term capital gains on sale of STT-paid listed equity shares of E Ltd.: ₹4,60,000;
- Short-term capital gains on sale of unlisted equity shares of M Ltd.: ₹4,68,000.
Applying the rates prescribed under Section 115AD as amended by the Finance (No. 2) Act, 2024, compute the total tax liability (including Health and Education Cess @ 4%) of STYLE Inc. for Assessment Year 2026-27.

Mark Wood, a foreign cricketer and citizen of the UK (non-resident in India), visited India during P.Y. 2025-26 to participate in a cricket tournament. He earned the following incomes:
- Match fees for participating in matches in India: ₹15,00,000;
- Fee for contributing articles regarding match analysis in an Indian sports magazine: ₹2,00,000;
- Brand endorsement fees for an Indian sports drink advertisement filmed in Mumbai: ₹10,00,000;
- Expenses incurred on training, travel, and agent commission: ₹4,00,000.
Assuming tax has been properly deducted at source under Section 194E, what is the tax treatment under Section 115BBA and is Mark Wood required to file a return of income under Section 139(1)?
